@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+`npm start` runs the dependency-free Node HTTP server on `PORT` (default 11222).
+`npm run build` performs syntax checks; `npm test` exercises the request handler
+in memory without starting a server or importing server.js.
+
+## Audit findings and changes
+
+- The previous logout response only displayed text. GET or POST `/logout` and
+  `/logout/` now return a confirmation, expire incoming root-path host cookies,
+  and send `Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache"`. Responses are not
+  cached. HEAD is read-only. Cross-site and same-site sibling-origin logout
+  requests with Fetch Metadata are rejected.
+- No login, session store, authentication provider, client-side JavaScript,
+  unsafe DOM sinks, window messaging, popups, embeds, or third-party executable
+  dependencies exist. Logout therefore clears browser state; if authentication
+  is added, revoke the server session/provider token before returning success.
+- Inline styles and the style attribute were moved to `/styles.css`. The markup
+  retains the existing design and navigation. Existing page links retain their
+  prior landing-page behavior; unknown URLs now return 404.
+- CSP defaults to denying resources, disables all scripts and inline handlers,
+  allows local styles and only the existing Unsplash image origin, denies plugin
+  content, base tags, framing, and outbound connections, and limits forms to this
+  origin. Trusted Types sink enforcement is enabled with no policy creation.
+  No permissive default policy or sanitizer dependency is needed because the site
+  does not insert HTML or execute JavaScript. Unsupported browsers still receive
+  CSP protections; the site itself requires no Trusted Types API.
+- All responses include framing, MIME sniffing, referrer, permissions, opener,
+  resource isolation, cache, and initial short-lived HSTS protections. Fetch
+  Metadata denies cross-origin resource requests while permitting incoming
+  document navigation. Missing metadata remains compatible with older clients.
+- The sole external asset is an existing mutable Unsplash background image. It is
+  narrowly allowed by CSP and receives no referrer. There are no CDN scripts or
+  styles requiring SRI; assigning a stable integrity hash to the mutable image
+  URL would break image delivery as the service changes its bytes.
+- Modern Reporting API and legacy CSP reports go to `/security-reports`.
+  Collection accepts at most 16 KiB, processes at most 50 reports, and logs only
+  counts using fixed directive names. URLs, queries, script samples, referrers,
+  credentials, and raw reports are discarded. Reports are untrusted signals,
+  never authorization or proof of an attack.
+
+## Deployment and future policy changes
+
+Terminate HTTPS at the hosting reverse proxy. Clear-Site-Data and browser reporting
+need a secure context and browser support. Cookie expiry is only a fallback for
+root-path host cookies; it cannot enumerate cookie Domain/Path scopes or erase
+all storage on unsupported browsers. Clear-Site-Data cookie clearing can also
+cover related subdomains: deploy on a dedicated site boundary as appropriate.
+
+Start with `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300`, confirm HTTPS operation, then
+increase the duration. Subdomain coverage and preload are intentionally excluded
+until every relevant hostname is HTTPS-ready. Do not cache HTML/logout responses
+or override these headers at the proxy. Apply proxy request-rate limits to the
+report collector; application logs already bound and scrub each report batch.
+
+The audited static page can enforce CSP immediately because it has no scripts,
+HTML sinks, or unknown integrations. For future changes, keep the current enforced
+policy and deploy a proposed stricter policy in
+`Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`, referencing the same reporting endpoint.
+Collect representative production traffic over days or weeks, filter extension
+noise, fix real incompatibilities, then promote the policy. Use fresh per-response
+nonces or release-time script hashes if scripts become necessary; do not add broad
+script origin allowlists or unsafe-inline/eval. Preserve reporting after promotion.
+
+COOP uses `same-origin-allow-popups` to avoid unnecessarily breaking future outbound
+OAuth/payment popups. COEP and full cross-origin isolation are omitted because no
+SharedArrayBuffer or shared-memory feature requires them and the external image
+has not been audited for those stronger constraints.
+
+Validation is automated and in memory only; no browser checks or servers were run.
